[section:intro Introduction]

Boost.Taar is a portable, header-only C++23 library that provides building
blocks for writing HTTP servers on top of __Asio__, __Beast__,
__Url__, and __Json__.

The HTTP layer is organised around four ideas:

* A [link boost_taar.intro.matchers /matcher DSL/] for selecting which
  handler should serve a given HTTP request.
* A [link boost_taar.intro.handlers /handler/] family that adapts ordinary
  callables into HTTP responders, with automatic argument extraction
  from path templates, query parameters, headers, cookies, and message
  bodies, and automatic injection of per-connection or per-session state.
* An [link boost_taar.intro.session HTTP session coroutine] that owns the
  request/response lifecycle for one connection, dispatching to registered
  matcher + handler pairs and reporting soft and hard errors through
  pluggable handlers.
* A [link boost_taar.intro.server TCP server coroutine] that accepts
  connections and spawns an HTTP session per connection, integrated with
  Asio's cancellation machinery.

On top of that transport sit three optional
[link boost_taar.intro.layers protocol layers] that reuse the same
callables and the same state objects: a JSON-RPC 2.0 endpoint, a Model
Context Protocol (MCP) server endpoint, and an OpenAPI document generator.

The library is header-only and does not require linking against a Taar
binary. It does depend on the compiled Boost libraries that __Asio__,
__Beast__, __Url__, __Json__, and __System__ require.

[section:matchers Matchers]

A matcher is a small object that inspects an HTTP request and answers the
question /does this request belong to me?/. Matchers compose with the usual
boolean operators (`&&`, `||`, `!`) and with relational operators
(`==`, `!=`) where it makes sense:

    method == http::verb::get && target == "/api/sum/{a}/{b}"

Every matcher also carries a compile-time description of the constraints
it imposes, which is what lets the OpenAPI generator document a route
without re-parsing the expression.

[endsect]

[section:handlers Handlers]

A handler turns an HTTP request into an HTTP response. The library ships
with two adapters:

* `taar::handler::rest` wraps a plain callable. Arguments declared on the
  callable are produced by /argument providers/ such as `path_arg`,
  `query_arg`, `header_arg`, `cookie_arg`, `string_body_arg`,
  `json_body_arg` and `url_encoded_form_data_arg`. The return value of
  the callable is converted into an HTTP response via the
  `tag_invoke`-based `response_from` extension point, so user-defined
  return types are supported out of the box.
* `taar::handler::htdocs` serves static documents from a root directory,
  with content-type inferred from the file extension.

You can also supply a hand-written callable with the signature
`(request, context) -> http::message_generator`, or one returning a
`chunked_response` / `async_generator` for streaming.

[heading Contexts]

A callable may start with parameters of the form `T&`, `T const&` or
`std::shared_ptr<T>` where `T` is a /context type in scope/. Those
parameters are not fed by argument providers; the library injects the
matching object:

* the /connection context/ of a `taar::session::basic_http<T>`, one
  object per TCP connection, shared by every request on it;
* a /keyed context/ added by `taar::handler::with_session`, looked up in
  a `taar::context_store` by a cookie or header value, with idle expiry
  and per-key serialisation;
* in the MCP layer, the /session/ object identified by `Mcp-Session-Id`.

Asking for a type that is not in scope is a compile-time error at the
registration site, so a handler cannot silently run without the state it
expects.

[endsect]

[section:session Sessions]

The `taar::session::http` coroutine holds a list of /(matcher, handler)/
pairs registered through `register_request_handler`. For each incoming
request the session walks the list, runs the first matching handler,
writes the response, and continues to the next request on the same
keep-alive connection.

The session distinguishes /soft errors/ (recoverable, application-visible
problems like a missing query parameter that can be turned into a 400
response) from /hard errors/ (connection-level problems that take the
connection down). Both have pluggable handlers, defaulting to sane
behaviour. A known path asked with the wrong method gets `405` with an
`Allow` header, and `HEAD` is served from the matching `GET` route.

`register_request_handler` accepts trailing /registration observers/,
callables invoked once with the route's matcher metadata and handler.
This is how `openapi::generator::describe` learns about a route.

[endsect]

[section:server Servers]

The `taar::server::tcp` coroutine binds, listens, and accepts connections,
spawning one HTTP session per connection. It integrates with Asio
cancellation signals so a single signal can shut down the whole server.

[endsect]

[section:layers Protocol layers]

Each layer is an ordinary object you own. It keeps its own registry of
callables and hands you a handler to register on one HTTP route.

* `taar::jsonrpc::endpoint<Context...>` implements JSON-RPC 2.0 on a
  single `POST` route: named parameters bound with `rpc_arg`, batches,
  notifications (answered `202`), the standard error codes, and a
  replaceable mapping from exceptions to error objects.
* `taar::mcp::endpoint<Session, Context...>` implements the Model Context
  Protocol over Streamable HTTP by composing a JSON-RPC endpoint: tools
  with input schemas derived from parameter types, resources and URI
  templates, the `initialize` lifecycle, sessions identified by
  `Mcp-Session-Id`, and a server-to-client event stream fed by
  `notify` and `broadcast`.
* `taar::openapi::generator` records REST routes at registration time and
  renders an OpenAPI 3.1 document from the matcher metadata, the argument
  providers and the callable's return type.

The same domain function can serve all three surfaces; each layer maps
its arguments and its exceptions natively, so domain code stays
protocol-agnostic. See [link boost_taar.design.layers the design notes]
for the rationale.

[endsect]

[endsect]
